cartridge "HIDDEN CAVERN/J.C.HILTY/1986"

' HIDDEN CAVERN - Land of the Cave Globs
' A ColecoVision game based on "Hidden Cavern" by James C. Hilty, Ahoy!
' April 1986 (Commodore 64 BASIC with a machine-code character scroller
' and sprite mover in DATA lines; the author's name is in the listing's
' REM lines). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic),
' 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from the
' listing, orig/hiddencavern.txt), the game is built for the ColecoVision:
' a banded red cave with dripping ceilings and crystal floors that slides
' smoothly one pixel at a time, a grey cave cruiser, green cave globs and
' loose rock spikes as smooth sprites, sound effects.
'
' The game: your cave cruiser holds the right side of a cavern that keeps
' flowing towards it. Steer it up and down to stay clear of the rock, the
' cave globs and the loose spikes that slide along the ceiling and the
' floor, and torpedo the globs.
'
' From the original: the cruiser sits at the right and moves only up and
' down; the cavern scrolls past it, faster after 500 points and faster
' again after 2500; two cave globs bounce about the cave; two stalactites
' slide along the ceiling and two stalagmites along the floor (here one of
' each, see below); touching rock, a glob or a spike loses a ship and
' starts the cave again; three ships. The button fires a torpedo to the
' left (one at a time): a glob hit is worth 250 and the glob comes back at
' the left; a torpedo that reaches the left edge is a MISS, and three
' misses end the game as surely as losing three ships. A point for every
' step of the scroll. GAME OVER says which of the two ended it, the score
' and the high score.
'
' Liberties: the cave is drawn as a smooth pixel scroll of a random cave
' (the C64 scrolled a fixed screen picture a character at a time): the
' ceiling and floor wander a row at a time and always leave at least
' seven rows of air; the cave moves 3/4 pixel a frame, 1 after 500 points
' and 1 1/2 after 2500, a point every 8 pixels; the C64 stopped the whole
' game while a torpedo flew (only the torpedo moved): here only the globs
' hold still (so aiming is as on the C64), the cave and the spikes keep
' going and the cruiser can still steer; the torpedo flies 6 pixels a
' frame; one ceiling spike and one floor spike instead of two of each
' (sprites per line), and they hug the rock under them and slide 1 1/2
' times as fast as the cave; the globs bounce inside the cave's width up
' to the cruiser (one 1/2 pixel a frame across and up, the other 1/2
' across and 1 down, after the C64's 2,-2 and 1,3), wobble slowly and
' come back at the left after a short pause; collisions are box tests on
' the drawn shapes; the score, high score, ships and misses are shown during play; GAME OVER then the
' title (the C64 asked PLAY AGAIN Y OR N); a title screen, credits and a
' title tune.
'
' Standing still cannot last: the ceiling and floor keep wandering, the
' spikes sweep every height next to the rock and the globs bounce up to
' the cruiser's column; firing at random runs into the three-miss limit.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): stick up and down steer the cruiser,
' either button fires. On the title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.
' Own xorshift random numbers.

